<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uesugi Kagekatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1556">1556</a>–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uesugi Kagekatsu was the adopted heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, who won the succession war after Kenshin's death in 1578. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> he was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> with large holdings. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600, and after the Tokugawa victory his lands were cut to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> Domain, which his descendants ruled until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu.jpg" alt="Uesugi Kagekatsu" width="320" height="775" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uesugi_Kagekatsu_Portrait_Yonezawa_City_Uesugi_Museum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kagekatsu was the son of Nagao Masakage, head of the Ueda Nagao clan, and of Aya-Gozen, the elder sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, who adopted him after his father's death; his childhood name was Unomatsu. He fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tedorigawa">Battle of Tedorigawa</a> in 1577. After Kenshin died, he defeated his rival, Kenshin's other adopted son Uesugi Kagetora, at the Siege of Otate, forced him to commit suicide in 1579 and became head of the clan. He then married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, the sister of Takeda Katsuyori.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The succession war let the generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a>, take the Uesugi lands in Kaga, Noto and Etchu. In 1582 Kagekatsu was defeated at the Battle of Tenjinyama, and Oda forces besieged Uozu castle, which fell on 3 June 1582. Writing to his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satake-yoshishige">Satake Yoshishige</a>, he said he would fight more than 60 provinces with Echigo alone. Nobunaga's death eighteen days later gave the Uesugi a reprieve.</p><p>Kagekatsu then sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, attacking Shibata's outposts in 1583 and serving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> campaign of 1590, and became one of the Council of Five Elders. In 1598 he exchanged his 550,000 koku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in Echigo for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a>, worth 1.2 million koku. After Hideyoshi's death he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>. His building of a new castle in Aizu and refusal to explain himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> helped begin the Sekigahara Campaign of 1600; in the north Mogami Yoshiaki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> fought him at Shiroishi and Hasedo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat he submitted to the Tokugawa and became a tozama daimyō with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> domain of 300,000 koku. He fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> in the Osaka Campaign of 1614 and 1615. He died at Yonezawa in 1623 and was succeeded by his illegitimate son Uesugi Sadakatsu. His remains were laid at the Shojoshin-in temple on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-koya">Mount Kōya</a>, and his ashes, court dress and helmet at the Uesugi mausoleum in Yonezawa. His tachi Yamatorige, a 13th-century blade, is a National Treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uesugi Kagekatsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kenshin died in 1578; his adopted son <b>Uesugi Kagekatsu</b> won the succession war and served Toyotomi Hideyoshi, who moved him to Aizu in 1598.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Uesugi Kagekatsu</b> opposed Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600 and in 1601 was stripped of his vast Aizu fief, keeping only Yonezawa with 300,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/yonezawa-domain">Yonezawa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.56,139.05&amp;year=1599" aria-label="Echigo Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8024 812 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Uesugi Kagekatsu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8474 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8474 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.56,139.05&amp;year=1599">What was here in 1599? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
